  "snippets": [
    {
      "title": "Blink the on-board LED on PC13",
      "toolchain": "CMSIS, no HAL",
      "lang": "c",
      "code": "#include \"stm32f1xx.h\"\n\n/* 1. Clock the port. Nothing else works until this line runs. */\nRCC->APB2ENR |= RCC_APB2ENR_IOPCEN;\n\n/* 2. Configure PC13. Pins 8-15 live in CRH, 4 bits each,\n      so PC13 sits at bit (13 - 8) * 4 = 20.\n      MODE = 10 (output, 2 MHz), CNF = 00 (push-pull). */\nGPIOC->CRH &= ~(0xFu << 20);\nGPIOC->CRH |=  (0x2u << 20);\n\n/* 3. BSRR writes without a read-modify-write, so it is\n      interrupt-safe. The LED is active low on this board. */\nGPIOC->BSRR = GPIO_BSRR_BR13;   /* LED on  */\nGPIOC->BSRR = GPIO_BSRR_BS13;   /* LED off */",
      "gotcha": "The peripheral clock enable in RCC is the step everyone misses. Without it the GPIO registers read and write as zero and the pin simply never moves, with no error anywhere."
    }
  ]
